1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,667
Hi — I'm Divij Pawar. This is part four of the STEM

2
00:00:02,833 --> 00:00:03,633
Agents series.

3
00:00:03,967 --> 00:00:04,767
Today:

4
00:00:04,533 --> 00:00:07,733
how do you actually design an agentic AI workflow — how to

5
00:00:07,733 --> 00:00:10,667
find the one piece that's really the model's job, how to choose

6
00:00:10,667 --> 00:00:11,833
your orchestration pattern,

7
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:15,133
and how to derive guardrails instead of copying someone else's.

8
00:00:15,740 --> 00:00:19,073
Traditional software writes an if-then rule for every case you can imagine

9
00:00:19,207 --> 00:00:21,673
— because the real world doesn't fit into if-else statements.

10
00:00:22,307 --> 00:00:25,973
Agentic engineering flips that: instead of scripting the whole problem, you isolate

11
00:00:25,973 --> 00:00:29,107
the one part that's genuinely unstructured, and hand only that part to

12
00:00:29,107 --> 00:00:29,907
a model.

13
00:00:29,807 --> 00:00:32,273
Here's the test: can you write a truth table for this decision?

14
00:00:32,573 --> 00:00:34,640
If yes, it's deterministic code — write the

15
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:38,207
if-else. If the boundary itself is fuzzy — a visual judgment call,

16
00:00:38,807 --> 00:00:42,707
an ambiguous sentence, an incomplete instruction — that's the model's job, and

17
00:00:42,707 --> 00:00:46,373
everything else stays engineered: the boundaries, the tools, the state.

18
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:49,487
Once you know which piece is the model's job, there's a second

19
00:00:49,487 --> 00:00:51,587
decision: who decides what happens next?

20
00:00:51,953 --> 00:00:54,853
One option is an autonomous agent loop — the model itself decides

21
00:00:54,853 --> 00:00:57,087
which tool to call, in what order, and when to stop.

22
00:00:57,553 --> 00:00:59,787
It's flexible, and it's how a lot of demos get built.

23
00:01:00,153 --> 00:01:03,487
The other option is a fixed, deterministic pipeline — the engineer decides

24
00:01:03,487 --> 00:01:06,687
the order in advance, and the model only reasons inside each step.

25
00:01:07,053 --> 00:01:07,853
It's less flexible,

26
00:01:08,453 --> 00:01:11,987
and it's what most production systems touching real consequences actually use.

27
00:01:12,553 --> 00:01:15,153
The case study we're about to walk through picks the boring option,

28
00:01:15,153 --> 00:01:17,253
on purpose: perception, then location,

29
00:01:17,753 --> 00:01:20,420
then grounding, then synthesis, always in that order.

30
00:01:20,787 --> 00:01:24,153
Predictability beats flexibility the moment you're touching legal or government

31
00:01:24,153 --> 00:01:24,953
systems.

32
00:01:25,430 --> 00:01:29,230
Here's a real case study: an open-source Android app called Pothole Reporter.

33
00:01:29,663 --> 00:01:33,063
It handles three kinds of civic reports — potholes, garbage, and open

34
00:01:33,063 --> 00:01:36,630
manholes — across several Indian states and every mapped national highway.

35
00:01:37,063 --> 00:01:39,263
But only one of those three actually uses AI.

36
00:01:39,863 --> 00:01:42,830
Garbage and manhole reports are just what the user says they are

37
00:01:42,930 --> 00:01:44,663
— a person looked and confirmed it.

38
00:01:45,163 --> 00:01:48,663
Potholes are different: judging whether road damage is bad enough to report

39
00:01:48,663 --> 00:01:51,863
is the fuzzy call the app hands to a vision model. A

40
00:01:51,863 --> 00:01:52,963
photo isn't an agent.

41
00:01:53,330 --> 00:01:56,563
An agent bridges raw perception and contextualized action.

42
00:01:56,997 --> 00:01:59,230
Let's reverse-engineer how this one does it.

43
00:01:59,780 --> 00:02:03,147
Step one, when you approach a problem as an agentic engineer: isolate

44
00:02:03,147 --> 00:02:05,613
the unstructured variable — the part you genuinely can't

45
00:02:06,113 --> 00:02:09,480
hardcode. Here, it's the physical environment. A pothole looks different in rain,

46
00:02:09,480 --> 00:02:12,980
at night, from any angle, so a vision-language model handles perception: find

47
00:02:13,080 --> 00:02:15,847
it, size it, call it clear, probable, uncertain, or absent.

48
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:19,580
Notice how narrow that job is — the model's blast radius here

49
00:02:19,580 --> 00:02:21,747
is just "does this look like reportable damage,"

50
00:02:22,347 --> 00:02:25,413
never "who's liable for it." The narrower the question you hand the

51
00:02:25,413 --> 00:02:28,213
model, the easier it is to catch it being wrong. But perception

52
00:02:28,213 --> 00:02:31,380
alone isn't enough. The model has no local context — it doesn't

53
00:02:31,380 --> 00:02:34,113
know whose road this actually is, or who's supposed to fix it.

54
00:02:34,547 --> 00:02:37,113
Guess at an authority from a photo alone, and you risk naming

55
00:02:37,113 --> 00:02:39,347
the wrong office, which is worse than naming none.

56
00:02:39,747 --> 00:02:43,513
That context gap gets closed with tools and retrieval, not more prompting.

57
00:02:44,110 --> 00:02:46,510
So instead of letting the model decide what to do next, this

58
00:02:46,510 --> 00:02:50,577
workflow runs a strict, deterministic pipeline — the engineer enforces the order.

59
00:02:51,210 --> 00:02:54,610
First, the photo: Drive mode runs a continuous background scan through the

60
00:02:54,610 --> 00:02:55,410
phone's camera,

61
00:02:55,643 --> 00:02:58,677
and nearby repeat sightings of the same pothole get folded into one

62
00:02:58,677 --> 00:02:59,543
report instead of

63
00:02:59,977 --> 00:03:02,843
ten. The frame goes to the vision model, which judges the damage

64
00:03:02,843 --> 00:03:06,310
and reports it as clear, probable, uncertain, or absent — never a

65
00:03:06,310 --> 00:03:07,577
fake precise percentage.

66
00:03:08,177 --> 00:03:08,977
Second,

67
00:03:09,077 --> 00:03:12,843
tool use: the agent grabs the exact GPS coordinate and makes a

68
00:03:12,843 --> 00:03:14,077
deterministic call to

69
00:03:14,843 --> 00:03:17,510
OpenStreetMap, reverse-geocoding it into a street address.

70
00:03:18,350 --> 00:03:21,717
Third, knowledge grounding — and this step only runs for supported Karnataka

71
00:03:21,750 --> 00:03:24,117
routes; nothing is invented for the other states.

72
00:03:24,717 --> 00:03:28,217
The system ranks the coordinate against a downloadable pack of thirteen thousand,

73
00:03:28,250 --> 00:03:32,150
five hundred seventy-seven contracts, itself filtered down from a public procurement

74
00:03:32,150 --> 00:03:35,983
snapshot of over forty-two thousand rows, and shortlists up to twenty-five

75
00:03:35,983 --> 00:03:36,783
candidates.

76
00:03:37,117 --> 00:03:40,350
A model may then pick one above a confidence threshold — and

77
00:03:40,350 --> 00:03:43,083
most of those contracts don't even have a known contractor name on

78
00:03:43,083 --> 00:03:46,217
file, so the app leaves that field blank rather than invent one.

79
00:03:46,783 --> 00:03:50,517
Fourth, action synthesis: the photo, the coordinate, the road-ownership check, and

80
00:03:50,517 --> 00:03:53,283
any contract match feed back into the model with a strict prompt

81
00:03:53,283 --> 00:03:56,517
template. Out comes one complaint, addressed to the right recipient.

82
00:03:56,983 --> 00:03:59,383
But the app never sends it — it opens your own email,

83
00:03:59,650 --> 00:04:00,883
filled in, and stops there.

84
00:04:01,490 --> 00:04:04,490
A good agentic engineer designs for failure, not just success.

85
00:04:05,157 --> 00:04:07,990
But don't just copy someone else's guardrails — derive your own by

86
00:04:07,990 --> 00:04:09,023
asking three questions.

87
00:04:09,423 --> 00:04:11,957
Where can your retrieval be wrong or stale? Hedge the language there.

88
00:04:12,257 --> 00:04:15,057
Where is the action irreversible or high-stakes? Put a human in front

89
00:04:15,057 --> 00:04:17,657
of it. And is there a category of input where refusing outright

90
00:04:17,657 --> 00:04:18,590
is safer than guessing?

91
00:04:18,957 --> 00:04:20,357
Build a fail-safe branch for it.

92
00:04:20,723 --> 00:04:23,623
This case study answers all three the same way: fail closed.

93
00:04:24,190 --> 00:04:25,957
Every downloaded dataset is checked

94
00:04:26,323 --> 00:04:29,423
byte-for-byte against a pinned checksum before it's trusted — if a file

95
00:04:29,423 --> 00:04:32,357
is missing or altered, that route refuses rather than guesses.

96
00:04:33,157 --> 00:04:36,023
On a national highway, the app refuses to name a maintaining authority

97
00:04:36,023 --> 00:04:38,923
itself and hands off to the official highway channel instead.

98
00:04:39,423 --> 00:04:42,957
A contract match is only ever labeled probable, because the actual warranty

99
00:04:42,957 --> 00:04:45,657
term isn't in the data — the app only infers it from

100
00:04:45,657 --> 00:04:46,890
how old the tender is.

101
00:04:47,423 --> 00:04:49,057
And nothing is ever sent automatically:

102
00:04:49,557 --> 00:04:52,623
the app doesn't call a complaint API and doesn't log in anywhere.

103
00:04:53,123 --> 00:04:55,490
It opens your own email, filled in, and stops.

104
00:04:56,320 --> 00:04:59,087
Here's the mistake that undoes all of this: trusting the model to

105
00:04:59,087 --> 00:05:00,120
grade its own homework.

106
00:05:00,587 --> 00:05:03,253
It's tempting to skip the human review step because the model is

107
00:05:03,253 --> 00:05:06,320
usually right — but usually right is exactly the failure mode you're

108
00:05:06,320 --> 00:05:07,520
designing guardrails against.

109
00:05:07,987 --> 00:05:10,453
The one case in a hundred where it's confidently wrong is the

110
00:05:10,453 --> 00:05:13,453
one that reaches a real commissioner's inbox, or a real bank account.

111
00:05:13,853 --> 00:05:16,453
If a guardrail only fires when you remember to check it, it

112
00:05:16,453 --> 00:05:18,053
isn't a guardrail — it's a suggestion.

113
00:05:18,700 --> 00:05:22,167
So — isolate the one genuinely unstructured piece, and run the truth-table

114
00:05:22,167 --> 00:05:23,167
test to find it.

115
00:05:23,567 --> 00:05:27,200
Choose a fixed pipeline over an autonomous loop wherever consequences are real.

116
00:05:27,633 --> 00:05:31,333
Derive your guardrails from three questions: what's stale, what's irreversible, what

117
00:05:31,333 --> 00:05:32,267
should refuse outright.

118
00:05:32,633 --> 00:05:35,533
And never let "usually right" replace a human check — that's the

119
00:05:35,533 --> 00:05:37,767
exact failure mode guardrails exist for.

120
00:05:38,410 --> 00:05:41,177
Your turn. Pick one AI feature you're building, or one you use

121
00:05:41,177 --> 00:05:41,977
every day.

122
00:05:42,177 --> 00:05:44,843
Run the truth-table test on what the model actually decides.

123
00:05:45,343 --> 00:05:47,743
Then ask whether it should run as a fixed pipeline or a

124
00:05:47,743 --> 00:05:49,943
looser agent loop, given what's actually at stake.

125
00:05:50,577 --> 00:05:54,310
Then derive one guardrail from what's stale, what's irreversible, or what should

126
00:05:54,310 --> 00:05:55,843
refuse outright — before you ship.

127
00:05:56,480 --> 00:05:57,280
Signing off — Divij Pawar.
